<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chengdu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chengdu is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> province in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, on the fertile Chengdu plain. It has been the political centre of the region since the ancient state of Shu and was the capital of several regional regimes, including Shu Han in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-period">Three Kingdoms period</a> and the Former and Later Shu in the 10th century. The nearby Dujiangyan irrigation system, built in the 3rd century BCE, made the plain one of China's richest farming areas.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chengdu.jpg" alt="Chengdu" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: FISU · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E9%9B%AA%E5%B1%B1%E4%B8%8B%E7%9A%84%E6%88%90%E9%83%BD%E5%B8%82%E5%A4%A9%E9%99%85%E7%BA%BF_Chengdu_skyline_with_snow_capped_mountains.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Chengdu lies in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> on a vast plain with an elevation of 450 to 720 metres, crossed by the Jin River. The Chengdu Plain is known as the Country of Heaven and the Land of Abundance, a phrase the Shu Han minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhuge-liang">Zhuge Liang</a> used of the region. The Qin Mountains far to the north shield the city from cold Siberian winds, so its winters are milder than those of the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Archaeological finds at Sanxingdui and the Jinsha Site show that the surrounding area was inhabited more than four thousand years ago. In the time of the Xia, Shang and Zhou dynasties it formed a separate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-working">bronze-working</a> culture that, after partial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinicization">sinification</a>, became known to the Chinese as Shu. The city is said to have been founded by the Kingdom of Shu in the 4th century BCE. Qin conquered Shu in 316 BCE, and the settlement was re-founded by the Qin general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-yi">Zhang Yi</a>.</p><p>The name Chengdu is attested from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a>, and the city has been called the only major Chinese city to keep both its location and its name through the imperial, republican and communist eras, although it was briefly known as Xijing in the 17th century.</p><p>Under the Han, Chengdu brocade became fashionable throughout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and a Brocade Official was appointed to oversee its production and trade. After the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Eastern Han</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bei">Liu Bei</a> ruled Shu Han from Chengdu. Under the Tang it was considered the second most prosperous city in China after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangzhou">Yangzhou</a>, and the poets <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-bai">Li Bai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/du-fu">Du Fu</a> lived there; Du Fu's thatched cottage of 760 is commemorated by the Caotang, built in 1078.</p><p>In 1911 Chengdu's branch of the Railway Protection Movement helped trigger the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuchang-uprising">Wuchang Uprising</a>. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> refugees from eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> fleeing the Japanese settled there, and after the war the city was briefly the capital of the Nationalist government before it withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taipei">Taipei</a>. A railway to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongqing">Chongqing</a> was built in 1952.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Chengdu is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> province, with 20,937,757 inhabitants at the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. The nearby Dujiangyan irrigation system and Mount Qingcheng, an important Taoist site, are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Sites</a>. Among its landmarks are the Wuhou Shrine, built to honour <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhuge-liang">Zhuge Liang</a>, the Jinsha Site museum and the Daci Temple, where the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a> was initiated. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> recognised it in 2011 as a city of gastronomy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chengdu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Leading the Jin armies, he captured the Southern Yan capital at Guanggu by the spring of 410, retook <b>Chengdu</b> and the land of Shu by advancing along the Min River, and, after the death of the Later Qin emperor Yao Xin, defeated Later Qin and a Northern Wei…</blockquote><a href="/empire/liu-song">Liu Song · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Song issued the world's first government paper money, the Jiaozi, and government factories printed it in Huizhou, <b>Chengdu</b>, Hangzhou and Anqi; in 1175 the Hangzhou factory employed more than a thousand workers a day.</blockquote><a href="/empire/southern-song">Southern Song · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Shi Dakai, whose family Wei and Qin had killed, led his army west and was executed by slow slicing near <b>Chengdu</b> in 1863.</blockquote><a href="/movement/taiping-rebellion">Taiping Rebellion · People</a></li><li><blockquote>Emperor Xizong then abandoned Chang'an for <b>Chengdu</b>, and Huang entered the capital and declared himself emperor of a new state of Qi.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-22">22 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Shu emerged in the <b>Chengdu</b> plain from a long local tradition: the Sanxingdui culture, with its giant bronze masks and standing figure, and the later Jinsha site in Chengdu.</blockquote><a href="/state/ancient-shu">Ancient Shu</a></li><li><blockquote>After his death his son Li Xiong took <b>Chengdu</b> in 304, styling himself King of Chengdu, and in 306 proclaimed himself emperor of Cheng.</blockquote><a href="/state/cheng-han">Cheng Han</a></li></ul></section>
